<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harald Hardrada</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1015–<a href="/years/1066">1066</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Harald III of Norway, Harald Sigurdsson</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Harald Hardrada (Harald Sigurdsson) was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> from 1046 to 1066. A half-brother of Olaf II, he served for years in the Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangian-guard">Varangian Guard</a> before returning to share and then hold the Norwegian throne. He fought a long war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and was killed invading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-stamford-bridge">Battle of Stamford Bridge</a> in 1066.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harald-hardrada.jpg" alt="Harald Hardrada" width="320" height="310" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zeichner: C. I. Schive, Lithograf Bucher in Bergen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harold-III-Coin.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Harald was born in Ringerike in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, in 1015 or possibly 1016, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asta-gudbrandsdatter">Åsta Gudbrandsdatter</a> and her second husband <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigurd-syr">Sigurd Syr</a>, a petty king of Ringerike and one of the strongest and wealthiest chieftains of the Uplands. Through his mother he was the youngest of three half-brothers of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-ii-of-norway">Olaf Haraldsson</a>, later Saint Olaf. In 1030, aged fifteen, he fought beside Olaf at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-stiklestad">Battle of Stiklestad</a>, where they were defeated by forces loyal to Cnut. Harald escaped to a remote farm, crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> about a year after the battle.</p><p>Grand Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a> welcomed him and made him a captain of his forces. Around 1033 or 1034 Harald and about 500 men went on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangian-guard">Varangian Guard</a>. He was found fighting on nearly every frontier of the empire, against Arab pirates in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and probably against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snorri-sturluson">Snorri Sturluson</a> calls him leader over all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangians">Varangians</a>. He grew extremely rich and sent his wealth to Yaroslav for safekeeping.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Harald left Novgorod in early 1045 and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigtuna">Sigtuna</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> at the end of 1045 or early in 1046. In 1046 he joined Sweyn of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in raids on the Danish coast, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-the-good">Magnus the Good</a>, unwilling to fight his uncle, agreed to share the kingship, which Harald held alone from the following year. He married Tora Torbergsdatter of a powerful Norwegian family to secure domestic support. Harald developed a Norwegian currency and a coin economy, started trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and Ireland, and according to later sagas founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a>.</p><p>From 1048 until 1064 he raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> almost every year in a war against Sweyn. In 1049 he pillaged and burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedeby">Hedeby</a>, the most important Danish trade centre, which never recovered as a civil town. Although he won most engagements, he never occupied Denmark. His epithet harðráði has been translated as hard in counsel, tyrannical, ruthless, severe or resolute, and it is not clear it was used in his lifetime.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Tostig Godwinson invited Harald to claim the English throne, and in September 1066 Harald invaded northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> with 10,000 troops and 300 longships. He beat the forces of Northumbria and Mercia at the Battle of Fulford on 20 September, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> surrendered on 24 September. On 25 September Harold Godwinson's army surprised the Norwegians at Stamford Bridge, where Harald had brought only light armour, and Harald was killed early in the battle, struck in the throat by an arrow. His army was wiped out. His body was moved to Nidaros the next year. Historians often consider his death the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Harald Hardrada” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1038 a Byzantine army under George Maniaces, with a corps of Normans and Varangians including <b>Harald Hardrada</b>, crossed the strait of Messina and took Syracuse, but a Muslim counter-offensive won back all the cities lost.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-sicily">Emirate of Sicily · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Harald Hardrada</b> (r. 1046–1066), a veteran of the Byzantine Varangian Guard, fought a long war against Denmark before invading England, where he was killed at the Battle of Stamford Bridge in 1066.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Harald Hardrada</b>, king of Norway and a veteran of the Varangian Guard, invaded England and was killed fighting the army of Harold Godwinson at Stamford Bridge on 25 September; less than three weeks later William of Normandy, a descendant of Rollo, defeated Harold at Hastings.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Rival claims by Harold Godwinson, <b>Harald Hardrada</b> and William of Normandy led to two invasions and the Norman Conquest that autumn.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-5">5 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Harald Hardrada</b>, King of Norway, and his English ally Tostig Godwinson defeated the northern earls Edwin and Morcar at Fulford, south of York, in his bid for the English throne.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-20">20 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>King Harold of England surprised and destroyed the Norwegian army near York, killing <b>Harald Hardrada</b> and Tostig.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-25">25 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ringerike, in what is now <a href="/countries/norway">Norway</a>. Died in Stamford Bridge, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=60.21,10.15&amp;year=1048" aria-label="Ringerike on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4762 148 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Harald Hardrada"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5212 418h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5212 418h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=60.21,10.15&amp;year=1048">What was here in 1048? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/sigurd-syr"><span class="ph ph-person" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Sigurd Syr</span><span class="tp">Father &#183; d. c. 1018</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/asta-gudbrandsdatter"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/asta-gudbrandsdatter.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Åsta Gudbrandsdatter</span><span class="tp">Mother</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/olaf-iii-of-norway"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/olaf-iii-of-norway.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Olaf III of Norway</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; c. 1050–1093</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;That was but a little man, yet he sat firmly in his stirrups.&#8221;</p><footer>Words at Stamford Bridge, 1066, on learning that the horseman had been Harold of England (Snorri Sturluson, Heimskringla) &#183; tr. Samuel Laing, 1844 &#183; Snorri's saga, written about 1230 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/598/pg598-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…and they who stand in the first rank shall set the spear-shaft on the ground, and the spear-point against the horseman's breast, if he rides at them; and those who stand in the second rank shall set the spear-point against the horse's breast.&#8221;</p><footer>Orders to his army at Stamford Bridge, 1066 (Snorri Sturluson, Heimskringla) &#183; tr. Samuel Laing, 1844 &#183; Snorri's saga, written about 1230 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/598/pg598-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Thy father gave thee this ring, but he took the ring from my father for some trifling cause…&#8221;</p><footer>Words to his nephew King Magnus the Good (Snorri Sturluson, Heimskringla, Saga of Harald Hardrade) &#183; tr. Samuel Laing, 1844 &#183; Snorri Sturluson's account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/598/pg598-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The Englishmen shall have a hard fray of it before we give ourselves up for lost.&#8221;</p><footer>Words at Stamford Bridge, 1066 (Snorri Sturluson, Heimskringla, Saga of Harald Hardrade) &#183; tr. Samuel Laing, 1844 &#183; Snorri Sturluson's account, written a century and a half later &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/598/pg598-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Let us now fall upon some good sensible counsel; for it is not to be concealed that this is an hostile army and the king himself without doubt is here.&#8221;</p><footer>Words at Stamford Bridge, 1066, on sighting the English army (Snorri Sturluson, Heimskringla, Saga of Harald Hardrade) &#183; tr. Samuel Laing, 1844 &#183; Snorri Sturluson's account, written a century and a half later &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/598/pg598-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
